WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
A Moodle installation is a service design before it is a working page.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:24.440
The visible site depends on application code, a supported web server, a supported database, a supported PHP runtime with required extensions, protected data storage, a configuration file, secure transport, mail delivery, scheduled processing, backup storage, and a way to monitor failure.

3
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:31.420
Administrators do not need to become core developers to understand this architecture, but they do need to know which team owns each layer.

4
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.900
Version awareness is part of installation planning.

5
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:43.800
This course uses Moodle LMS 5.2 as the operating baseline, with the deployed branch verified against official documentation and release notes.

6
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.940
Requirements change over time, so remembered values from an older site are not reliable.

7
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:58.800
Moodle's Environment report is useful because it compares the current server environment against requirements for the installed version and, where supported, future upgrade targets.

8
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.400
Moodle 5.1 introduced a revised code-directory structure that includes a web-accessible public directory.

9
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:19.180
The operational lesson is simple: confirm that the web server exposes only the intended public application entry point, while configuration, generated files, uploaded learning content, temporary files, and backups remain protected.

10
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.800
Exposing the wrong directory is not a cosmetic mistake; it can change the site's risk profile.

11
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.640
Baseline configuration should be deliberate.

12
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:46.140
Site name, locale, default language, time zone, support contacts, outbound email, site policies, default user preferences, course defaults, session behavior, file limits, navigation, appearance, and optional advanced features all shape later administration.

13
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.240
Defaults reduce repetitive work when they match the organization.

14
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.780
They create support debt when they silently force the wrong design into every new course.

15
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.500
Environment separation keeps normal administration safer.

16
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.040
Production needs stability and change control.

17
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:11.140
Staging lets administrators test upgrades, themes, authentication changes, plugins, and integrations before learners depend on them.

18
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.360
Development environments help with experimentation.

19
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.020
Restore-test environments prove whether backups are actually usable.

20
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:27.120
Production data should not be copied casually into lower environments, especially when it contains personal information or assessment records.

21
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.720
Installation is not complete when the login page appears.

22
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:47.980
It is complete when baseline services are verified: cron or scheduled tasks are running, mail can be delivered and diagnosed, backups have a destination, restore testing is possible, support contacts are known, monitoring can detect failure, and administrative ownership is documented.

23
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.680
A clean installation creates an operating baseline that future changes can be compared against.
